Q--> 1. What is the difference between API and MCP (also write the drawback of API over MCP) in ddetails?

API (Application Programming Interface) and MCP (Model Context Protocol) are both used to connect software with external services, but their purpose is different.

API

An API is a set of rules that allows one software application to communicate with another application or service. For example, a weather application can use a Weather API to get the current temperature of a city.

Flow:
Application → API → External Service → Response

APIs are mainly designed for developers and applications.

MCP

MCP stands for Model Context Protocol. It is a standardized protocol designed for AI models and AI agents to connect with external tools, data, and services.

For example, an AI agent can use MCP to access files, databases, GitHub, web search, or other tools.

Flow:
AI Agent → MCP → Tool/Service

| API                                 | MCP                                          |
| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| General communication interface     | AI-focused communication protocol            |
| Mainly used by applications         | Mainly used by AI agents                     |
| Usually requires custom integration | Provides a standardized way to connect tools |
| Tool discovery is not standardized  | Supports tool discovery                      |
| Not specifically designed for AI    | Designed specifically for AI applications    |
| Examples: REST, GraphQL APIs        | MCP servers and MCP tools                    |



Important Point:

MCP does not replace APIs. In many cases, MCP works on top of APIs.